    "etc-q3",
    {
      "id": "etc-q3",
      "verified": "BEU Verified Model Answer · 7 Marks",
      "intro": "The Technical Writing Process is a recursive four-stage cognitive and communicative workflow—Pre-writing, Drafting, Revising, and Editing—transforming raw technical data and engineering concepts into clear, user-centric documentation.",
      "diagrams": [
        {
          "title": "The Recursive Technical Writing Workflow",
          "ascii": "    +-------------------------------------------------------+\n    | 1. PRE-WRITING                                        |\n    |    - Define purpose, analyze audience, gather data    |\n    |    - Brainstorming, mind mapping, structural outline  |\n    +---------------------------+---------------------------+\n                                |\n                                v\n    +-------------------------------------------------------+\n    | 2. DRAFTING                                           |\n    |    - Rapid rough draft creation without self-censoring|\n    |    - Focus on completeness, sequence, and technical logic\n    +---------------------------+---------------------------+\n                                |\n                                v\n    +-------------------------------------------------------+\n    | 3. REVISING (Macro-Level Overhaul)                    |\n    |    - Content re-structuring, flow, logical gaps       |\n    |    - Ensure audience alignment & technical accuracy   |\n    +---------------------------+---------------------------+\n                                |\n                                v\n    +-------------------------------------------------------+\n    | 4. EDITING & PROOFREADING (Micro-Level Polish)        |\n    |    - Grammar, punctuation, active voice, style guide  |\n    |    - Formatting, cross-references, typography checks  |\n    +-------------------------------------------------------+"
        }
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "1. In-Depth Analysis of the Four Stages",
          "points": [
            "<b>Stage 1: Pre-Writing (Planning & Research):</b> 40% of project time. Technical writers define document goals (inform, instruct, persuade), profile audience knowledge level, interview Subject Matter Experts (SMEs), inspect source code and schematics, and create structured modular outlines.",
            "<b>Stage 2: Drafting (Composing):</b> Rapid writing phase where thoughts are converted into continuous text without stopping for minor syntactic errors. Focus on chronological steps, logical flow, data precision, and preliminary diagram integration.",
            "<b>Stage 3: Revising (Substantive Reorganization):</b> High-level evaluation: Does the document fulfill its intended objective? Are explanations coherent? Delete redundant sections, insert missing prerequisites, reorganize sections for smoother transitions, and refine technical accuracy.",
            "<b>Stage 4: Editing and Proofreading:</b> Low-level scrutiny divided into: <i>Copyediting</i> (mechanics, spelling, punctuation, parallelism, passive-to-active voice conversion) and <i>Proofreading</i> (final visual layout check, broken links, figure numbers, font consistency)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "2. Distinction Between Revising and Editing",
          "points": [
            "<b>Revising is Macro:</b> Deals with content, structural logic, clarity, organization, and audience relevance.",
            "<b>Editing is Micro:</b> Deals with grammar, punctuation, sentence economy, terminology, and typographical correctness."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "conclusion": "Effective technical writing treats composition as an iterative engineering discipline, ensuring technical deliverables are structurally robust, factually verified, and linguistically polished.",
      "markingScheme": [
        {
          "component": "Overview & The Four Stages Defined",
          "marks": 1.5
        },
        {
          "component": "Detailed Activities in Each Phase (Pre-writing, Drafting, Revising, Editing)",
          "marks": 3.5
        },
        {
          "component": "Macro vs. Micro Distinction & Flowchart Diagram",
          "marks": 2
        }
      ],
      "examTip": "Clearly distinguish between \"Revising\" (macro-level restructuring) and \"Editing\" (micro-level grammar/mechanics). Draw the recursive 4-stage flowchart."
    }
